  "unitA1U01Title": "Saying who you are",
  "unitA1U01Outcome": "I can say who I am, where I live and how I am doing, and keep the verb in second position while I do it.",
  "unitA1U02Title": "Naming things",
  "unitA1U02Outcome": "I can name everyday objects with the right article, make them plural, and guess the gender of a word I have never seen from how it ends.",
  "unitA1U03Title": "Asking and refusing",
  "unitA1U03Outcome": "I can ask for what I need, say what is not the case, and choose between du and Sie without having to think about it.",
  "unitA1U04Title": "Objects and ownership",
  "unitA1U04Outcome": "I can say what I have, want and am buying, and say whose it is, without confusing the subject of the sentence with its object.",
  "unitA1U06Title": "Saying what you want to do",
  "unitA1U06Outcome": "I can say what I can, must and would like to do, say what I like doing, and tell someone else to do something.",
  "unitA1U07Title": "Getting around",
  "unitA1U07Outcome": "I can say where I am, where I am going and where I have come from, and use verbs like ankommen and abfahren with the prefix in its place.",
  "unitA1U08Title": "Times and quantities",
  "unitA1U08Outcome": "I can give a time, a date and an amount, and order time, manner and place in one sentence the way German expects.",
  "unitA1U09Title": "Joining ideas",
  "unitA1U09Outcome": "I can join two statements with und, aber, oder and denn, describe things with simple adjectives, and say what there is.",
  "unitA1U11Title": "Talking about yesterday",
  "unitA1U11Outcome": "I can tell someone what I did yesterday in the Perfekt, and I know the places where German uses no article at all.",
  "unitA2U01Title": "Telling what happened",
  "unitA2U01Outcome": "I can tell a short story about the past, choose haben or sein without guessing, and use war, hatte and konnte.",
  "unitA2U02Title": "Giving and receiving",
  "unitA2U02Outcome": "I can say who I gave something to, replace both objects with pronouns, and put those pronouns in the order German wants.",
  "unitA2U03Title": "Giving reasons",
  "unitA2U03Outcome": "I can explain why I do something, report what someone said with dass, and start a sentence with the reason instead of the main clause.",
  "unitA2U04Title": "Comparing",
  "unitA2U04Outcome": "I can compare two things, name the best of several, and put the usual endings on an adjective standing in front of a noun.",
  "unitA2U06Title": "Where things are, where they go",
  "unitA2U06Outcome": "I can say where something is and where it is being put, and say how long something has lasted and how long ago it happened.",
  "unitA2U07Title": "Your day, in detail",
  "unitA2U07Outcome": "I can describe my daily routine with reflexive verbs, say what we do to each other, and put nicht where I actually mean it.",
  "unitA2U08Title": "Purpose and intention",
  "unitA2U08Outcome": "I can say what I intend to do and why, ask a question indirectly to be polite, and tell als from wenn when talking about the past.",
  "unitA2U09Title": "Saying which one",
  "unitA2U09Outcome": "I can say which one I mean with a relative clause, and use dieser, jemand, niemand and meiner in the right case.",
  "unitA2U11Title": "Finishing touches",
  "unitA2U11Outcome": "I can use viele, einige and ein paar with the right adjective endings, and link ideas with sowohl … als auch and entweder … oder.",
  "unitB1U01Title": "Describing precisely",
  "unitB1U01Outcome": "I can put the right ending on any adjective, turn an adjective into a noun, and describe things with gekocht and kochend.",
  "unitB1U02Title": "Which one, and whose",
  "unitB1U02Outcome": "I can pin down exactly which person or thing I mean — including whose — with a relative clause in any case.",
  "unitB1U03Title": "Narrating the past",
  "unitB1U03Outcome": "I can narrate a sequence of past events, say what had already happened before them, and choose Perfekt or Präteritum to suit the register.",
  "unitB1U04Title": "Space and direction",
  "unitB1U04Outcome": "I can use the preposition a verb demands, refer back to a whole idea with darauf or worüber, and hang a prepositional phrase on a noun.",
  "unitB1U06Title": "Politeness and hypothesis",
  "unitB1U06Outcome": "I can make a request sound polite, say what I would do, and say that something looks as if it were the case.",
  "unitB1U07Title": "Who does what to whom",
  "unitB1U07Outcome": "I can say what is done without naming who does it, and tell the future, passive and become senses of werden apart.",
  "unitB1U08Title": "Word order under pressure",
  "unitB1U08Outcome": "I can keep the verb in the right place after any connector, and order pronouns in the middle of a clause without stopping to think.",
  "unitB1U09Title": "Saying it in fewer words",
  "unitB1U09Outcome": "I can express purpose, absence and substitution with an infinitive, say what need not be done, and link two rising quantities.",
  "unitB1U11Title": "Building new words",
  "unitB1U11Outcome": "I can read and build German compounds, and see how a prefix or a suffix turns one word into another.",
  "unitB2U01Title": "The passive, fully",
  "unitB2U01Outcome": "I can use the passive in any tense, add a modal verb to it, and tell an action being done from a state that has resulted.",
  "unitB2U02Title": "Saying it without the passive",
  "unitB2U02Outcome": "I can replace a passive with man, sich lassen or an -bar adjective, and assemble the verb cluster at the end of a clause correctly.",
  "unitB2U03Title": "Reporting what others said",
  "unitB2U03Outcome": "I can report speech at a distance in Konjunktiv I, and use soll and will to mark a claim as someone else’s rather than mine.",
  "unitB2U04Title": "Formal noun style",
  "unitB2U04Outcome": "I can write in the noun-heavy style German officialdom uses, decline the weak masculine nouns, and give a noun the complement it requires.",
  "unitB2U06Title": "Packing detail into a noun phrase",
  "unitB2U06Outcome": "I can read and write the long phrases that sit in front of a noun in written German, and keep agreement right when subjects are joined.",
  "unitB2U07Title": "Connecting an argument",
  "unitB2U07Outcome": "I can concede a point and then counter it, state means and result, and mark my own attitude with a sentence adverb.",
  "unitB2U08Title": "What might have been",
  "unitB2U08Outcome": "I can say what would have happened if, state a condition without wenn, and negate a whole series of things cleanly.",
  "unitB2U09Title": "Ordering the sentence",
  "unitB2U09Outcome": "I can decide what belongs at the front of a German sentence, hold a clause open with es or darauf, and tell an argument from an adjunct.",
  "unitB2U11Title": "Sounding German",
  "unitB2U11Outcome": "I can use doch, mal, ja and eben the way a native speaker does, and build the longer infinitive clauses of careful speech.",
  "unitC1U01Title": "Reporting a source",
  "unitC1U01Outcome": "I can report a source across several sentences without losing the thread, keeping tense and reference consistent throughout.",
  "unitC1U02Title": "Nominal style",
  "unitC1U02Outcome": "I can move between nominal and verbal style at will, and keep the arguments a nominalisation inherits from the verb it came from.",
  "unitC1U03Title": "Complex noun phrases",
  "unitC1U03Outcome": "I can unpack the longest pre-noun phrases in academic German, and insert an apposition or a parenthesis without breaking the sentence.",
  "unitC1U04Title": "Case in formal registers",
  "unitC1U04Outcome": "I can use the genitive where formal German still requires it, and choose the case after prepositions like trotz, dank and wegen.",
  "unitC1U06Title": "Relations between clauses",
  "unitC1U06Outcome": "I can express fine relations between clauses, and tell a relative clause that narrows a reference from one that merely adds to it.",
  "unitC1U07Title": "Word order as meaning",
  "unitC1U07Outcome": "I can put given and new information where German expects them, and move heavy material out past the end of the verb bracket.",
  "unitC1U08Title": "Stance and scope",
  "unitC1U08Outcome": "I can signal my stance precisely, control exactly what a negation covers, and read ist zu tun as the modal passive it is.",
  "unitC1U09Title": "Verb clusters and predicates",
  "unitC1U09Outcome": "I can order a three-verb cluster at the end of a clause, and build a counterfactual that spans two different times at once.",
  "unitC1U11Title": "The edges of the syntax",
  "unitC1U11Outcome": "I can use the several different es of German correctly, and recognise a verb-second clause embedded after a verb of saying or thinking.",
  "unitC2U01Title": "Particles and stance",
  "unitC2U01Outcome": "I can shade a statement with particles the way native speakers do, mark how I know something, and soften a claim without weakening it.",
  "unitC2U02Title": "Marked word order",
  "unitC2U02Outcome": "I can move an element to the front for emphasis, and build the focusing structures that answer which one exactly.",
  "unitC2U03Title": "Leaving things out",
  "unitC2U03Outcome": "I can omit what German allows to be omitted, in speech and in writing, without becoming ambiguous.",
  "unitC2U04Title": "Register and idiom",
  "unitC2U04Outcome": "I can rewrite the same content across registers, and choose the preposition an idiom demands rather than the one logic suggests.",
  "unitC2U06Outcome": "I can read the densest academic German and unpack it into clear sentences, and compress my own prose when the register asks for it.",
  "unitC2U07Title": "Perspective in narrative",
  "unitC2U07Outcome": "I can hold a narrative perspective across a whole passage, including free indirect speech and the historical present.",
  "unitC2U08Title": "Counterfactual and conditional",
  "unitC2U08Outcome": "I can express regret and hypothesis about the past, and use verb-first clauses for conditions, concessions and wishes.",
  "unitC2U09Title": "Scope and ambiguity",
  "unitC2U09Outcome": "I can see where a sentence could be read two ways and rewrite it so it cannot, including the scope of negation and quantifiers.",
  "unitC2U11Title": "The last corners",
  "unitC2U11Outcome": "I can use the constructions that live at the edges of the grammar: verbless adjuncts, stand-alone subordinate clauses, and spoken weil with the verb in second position.",
