  "guideTableA1SeinHabenTitle": "Present tense: sein and haben",
  "guideTableA1SeinHabenNote": "These two verbs are irregular and should be learned as complete paradigms.",
  "guideTableA1RegularPresentTitle": "Regular present tense: machen",
  "guideTableA1RegularPresentNote": "Remove -en from the infinitive to find the stem, then add the personal ending.",
  "guideTableA1ArticlesTitle": "Definite articles by case",
  "guideTableA1ArticlesNote": "The masculine accusative is the first visible case change most learners meet: der becomes den.",
  "guideTableA1ModalsTitle": "Modal verbs in the present",
  "guideTableA1ModalsNote": "The modal is conjugated in position two; the second verb stays in the infinitive at the end.",
  "guideTableA2PronounsTitle": "Personal pronouns by case",
  "guideTableA2PronounsNote": "Ask who performs the action, whom it affects, and to whom or for whom it happens.",
  "guideTableA2PerfectTitle": "Perfect tense: auxiliary and participle",
  "guideTableA2PerfectNote": "The conjugated auxiliary occupies position two; the participle closes the sentence bracket.",
  "guideTableA2TwoWayNote": "The case follows meaning, not the verb alone: location uses dative and directed placement uses accusative.",
  "guideTableB1AdjectiveEndingsTitle": "Adjective endings: the signal principle",
  "guideTableB1AdjectiveEndingsNote": "An adjective supplies the grammatical signal only when the word before it does not already supply it.",
  "guideTableB1RelativeTitle": "Relative pronouns",
  "guideTableB1RelativeNote": "Gender and number come from the antecedent; case comes from the relative pronoun’s job inside its own clause.",
  "guideTableB1PassiveTitle": "Process passive with werden",
  "guideTableB1PassiveNote": "Use werden for a process. sein + participle normally describes the resulting state.",
  "guideTableB2KonjunktivTwoTitle": "Konjunktiv II: unreal and polite language",
  "guideTableB2KonjunktivTwoNote": "Common short forms such as wäre, hätte and könnte are preferred to würde + infinitive.",
  "guideTableB2TenseOverviewTitle": "Tense and register overview",
  "guideTableB2TenseOverviewNote": "German tense choice depends on register and viewpoint as well as calendar time.",
  "guideTableC1IndirectSpeechTitle": "Konjunktiv I in reported speech",
  "guideTableC1IndirectSpeechNote": "When Konjunktiv I is identical to the indicative, formal German often substitutes a clear Konjunktiv II form.",
  "guideTableC2RegisterTitle": "Compact alternatives in formal register",
  "guideTableC2RegisterNote": "Nominal style can make formal prose denser, but too much of it reduces clarity. Choose it deliberately, not automatically.",
  "guideTableA1IndefiniteArticlesTitle": "Indefinite articles and kein",
  "guideTableA1IndefiniteArticlesNote": "Kein follows the ein-word endings. There is no positive indefinite article in the plural. The dative plural noun normally adds -n.",
  "guideTableA1WordOrderTitle": "Main-clause word order",
  "guideTableA1WordOrderNote": "In a statement the finite verb stays in position two; position one may hold the subject, time, place or another single constituent.",
  "guideTableA2StemChangingVerbsTitle": "Present-tense stem changes",
  "guideTableA2StemChangingVerbsNote": "The vowel change normally appears only with du and er/sie/es; the plural forms keep the infinitive stem.",
  "guideTableA2PrepositionCasesTitle": "Prepositions grouped by case",
  "guideTableA2PrepositionCasesNote": "Learn a preposition with its case as one item. Two-way prepositions use dative for location and accusative for a destination.",
  "guideTableA2ReflexivePronounsTitle": "Reflexive pronouns",
  "guideTableA2ReflexivePronounsNote": "Use the dative form when the clause already has another accusative object: Ich wasche mir die Hände.",
  "guideTableB1WeakAdjectiveEndingsTitle": "Adjective endings after der-words",
  "guideTableB1WeakAdjectiveEndingsNote": "After der/die/das and other der-words, the article already carries the grammatical signal. Only five cells use -e; all others use -en.",
  "guideTableB1SubordinateConnectorsTitle": "Subordinate-clause connectors",
  "guideTableB1SubordinateConnectorsNote": "A subordinating connector sends the finite verb to the end of its clause. The whole subordinate clause occupies one sentence position.",
  "guideTableB2PassiveContrastTitle": "Process passive and state passive",
  "guideTableB2PassiveContrastNote": "Werden presents an event or process. Sein presents the state that holds after an event.",
  "guideTableB2VerbPrepositionPairsTitle": "Common verb–preposition pairs",
  "guideTableB2VerbPrepositionPairsNote": "Treat the verb, preposition and case as one lexical unit; the preposition is not freely interchangeable with its English gloss.",
  "guideTableC1NominalisationTitle": "Clause and nominalisation alternatives",
  "guideTableC1NominalisationNote": "Nominalisation is useful in formal writing, but a verb-led clause is often clearer. Choose based on information density and register.",
  "guideTableC1DiscourseConnectorsTitle": "Connectors for argument structure",
  "guideTableC1DiscourseConnectorsNote": "Sentence adverbs occupy a position in the main clause; unlike subordinating conjunctions, they do not send the finite verb to the end.",
  "guideTableC2StanceAndHedgingTitle": "Calibrating claims in advanced prose",
  "guideTableC2StanceAndHedgingNote": "Advanced style does not mean making every claim weaker. Match the strength of the wording to the strength and source of the evidence.",
